---
title: "MCP Events"
description: "Experimental. Deliver events to MCP Events webhook subscriptions, signed with Standard Webhooks."
---

{% callout type="warning" %}
Experimental. This destination type and its behavior may change without notice. It is off unless [`MCP_ENABLED`](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration#mcp-events-experimental) is `true`, and is created and updated with the Admin API key only. Delete `mcp` destinations before turning it off; while it is off, they stay listed and each event fails once without retries.
{% /callout %}

An `mcp` destination is one MCP Events webhook subscription: a callback URL that receives one topic's events, wrapped in the MCP Events envelope and signed with [Standard Webhooks](https://www.standardwebhooks.com/). Your MCP server creates it on behalf of an agent and refreshes or deletes it as the agent subscribes, renews and unsubscribes.

## Creating an MCP Destination

```sh
curl '{% $OUTPOST_API_BASE_URL %}/tenants/<TENANT_ID>/destinations' \
--header 'Content-Type: application/json' \
--header 'Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>' \
--data '{
  "id": "sub_12345",
  "type": "mcp",
  "topics": ["order.created"],
  "config": {
    "url": "https://agent.example.com/mcp-events/abc123",
    "principal": "user-123"
  },
  "credentials": {
    "secret": "whsec_MfKQ9r8GKYqrTwjUPD8ILPZIo2LaLaSw"
  },
  "expires_at": "2030-01-01T00:00:00Z"
}'
```

Before the destination is stored, Outpost sends a verification challenge to the callback URL. If the callback fails it, the request returns `422` with the reason as the validation type: `challenge_failed`, `connection_refused`, `timeout`, `tls_error`, `http_4xx`, `http_5xx` or `https_required`. If Outpost hits a verification limit, the request returns `429` with a `Retry-After` header and `data.code` of `rate_limited`. If verification can't run, it returns `503` with `data.code` of `verification_unavailable`. A callback that resolves to a non-public address returns `config.url must resolve to a public address`. A callback that was already verified for the same tenant, principal and URL is not challenged again for `MCP_VERIFICATION_TTL_SECONDS`.

Verification is limited to 10 challenges per tenant and principal per minute, and 120 failed challenges per tenant and callback host per minute. Subscriptions without a principal share the tenant's budget.

## Configuration

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|-------|------|----------|-------------|
| `id` | string | No | Subscription ID, sent as `X-MCP-Subscription-Id`. 1 to 128 characters of `A-Z a-z 0-9 _ -`. |
| `topics` | string[] | Yes | Exactly one topic. `*` is not accepted. |
| `config.url` | string | Yes | Callback URL. `https`, in normalized form (lowercase host, no default port, no credentials); `http` only for loopback addresses in `DESTINATIONS_PRIVATE_ADDRESS_ALLOWLIST` (any allowlisted address with `MCP_ALLOW_INSECURE_CALLBACKS`). Must resolve to a public address, unless allowed by `DESTINATIONS_PRIVATE_ADDRESS_ALLOWLIST`. |
| `config.principal` | string | No | The subject the subscription belongs to, up to 512 bytes. Verification is cached per tenant, principal and URL. |
| `config.arguments` | string | No | Canonical JSON object, up to 4 KiB. Stored with the destination. With event definitions configured, it must satisfy the event's `inputSchema`. |
| `credentials.secret` | string | Yes | `whsec_` followed by base64 of 24 to 64 bytes. |
| `expires_at` | string | No | RFC 3339 time in the future. From then on the subscription matches no events and receives no deliveries. `null` on update clears it. |

`config.url`, `config.principal`, `config.arguments` and `topics` cannot change after creation. To rotate the secret, update `credentials.secret`: the old secret keeps signing for 24 hours alongside the new one.

Each subscription counts toward `MAX_DESTINATIONS_PER_TENANT`. When a tenant is at the limit, creating a subscription first removes the tenant's expired ones.

## Event Definitions and Arguments

With [`MCP_EVENTS_FILE` or `mcp.events`](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration#mcp-events-experimental) set, the configured events form a catalog. A subscription must then target an event in the catalog (`422` with `topics` / `not_found` otherwise), and `config.arguments` is checked against the event's `inputSchema`, which Outpost derives from its `payload_schema`: each top-level property with a scalar type becomes an optional argument that takes a value, a list of values, or, for numbers and `format: date` strings, an operator object (`$gt`, `$gte`, `$lt`, `$lte`). Set `x-mcp-filter: false` on a property to leave it out. Outpost turns the arguments into the destination's `filter`, so only matching events are delivered; `filter` can't be set on these subscriptions. Checks against the catalog run when a subscription is created. Subscriptions created before event definitions were configured keep delivering unfiltered until they are recreated.

`GET /mcp/events` (Admin API key) returns the catalog in the shape of an MCP `events/list` result (`resultType: "complete"`): `name`, `description`, `inputSchema`, `payloadSchema` and `delivery` per event, `nextCursor` when there are more. `topics=a,b` restricts the list to those events, and `limit` and `cursor` page through it. Without MCP enabled or any events configured, the route returns `404`.

## Delivery

Each event is sent as an HTTP POST:

```
POST /mcp-events/abc123 HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json
webhook-id: msg_x1yQ3o8mT5vZk2cJ0aNwXg
webhook-timestamp: 1790000000
webhook-signature: v1,K5oZfzN95Z9UVu1EsfQmfVNQhnkZ2/d+...
X-MCP-Subscription-Id: sub_12345

{"eventId":"evt_123","name":"order.created","timestamp":"2026-10-27T12:00:00Z","data":{"order_id":"ord_42"},"cursor":null}
```

- `cursor` is always `null`: there is no replay, and Outpost doesn't send `gap` or `terminated` envelopes.
- `webhook-id` identifies the message. It is the same on every retry and redelivery of an event to a subscription, and differs for each subscription and event, so an event matched by several subscriptions has a different ID on each. Dedupe on it per subscription.
- `webhook-timestamp` and `webhook-signature` are computed again on every attempt. During a secret rotation, the signature header carries one signature per valid secret.
- Redirects are never followed.
- Deliveries and verification challenges connect directly. `DESTINATIONS_PROXY_URL` and `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY` don't apply, so the address check covers the address actually connected to.

## Retries

A `2xx` response is a success. Any other response, a network error or a refused address is a failed attempt and is retried on `MCP_RETRY_SCHEDULE` (`RETRY_SCHEDULE` and `MAX_RETRY_LIMIT` don't apply to `mcp`). A `3xx` is a failure with its status as the attempt code.

These are not retried, and don't count toward auto-disabling the destination: a `410` or `413` response, and an event whose envelope exceeds 256 KiB (attempt code `payload_too_large`, no request sent).
